/-
# Non-passable varieties of groups (Kourovka Notebook Problem 8.26)

Kourovka Notebook, Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 8.26 (A. V. Kuznetsov): we call a variety
passable if there exists an unrefinable chain of its subvarieties which is well-ordered by
inclusion. Do there exist non-passable varieties of groups?
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.FreeGroup.Basic
public import WantedExt.GroupTheory.NilpotentClassFourVarietyLatticeKourovka3Problem53Wanted

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.NonPassableVarietyKourovka8Problem26Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 8.26 (A. V.
Kuznetsov). UnsolvedMath record UM-00700478 (`KOU-8.26`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

Subvarieties of the variety with laws `V` are given by their laws, the fully invariant subgroups `U
≥ V` of `FreeGroup ℕ` (`IsFullyInvariant` of Kourovka 3.53);
inclusion of varieties reverses inclusion of laws. An unrefinable chain of subvarieties is a maximal
chain of such `U`; it is well-ordered by inclusion of varieties when every nonempty subset has a
smallest variety, i.e. a largest set of laws.
-/

open MathlibExt.GroupTheory.NilpotentClassFourVarietyLatticeKourovka3Problem53Wanted
  (IsFullyInvariant)

/-- The variety with set of laws `V` is passable (A. V. Kuznetsov): some unrefinable (maximal)
chain of its subvarieties is well-ordered by inclusion. Subvarieties are given by their sets of
laws, the fully invariant subgroups `U ≥ V` of `FreeGroup ℕ`, a smaller variety having more laws;
so the chain `C` is a maximal chain of such `U` in which every nonempty subset has a largest
member (a smallest variety). -/
def IsPassable (V : Subgroup (FreeGroup ℕ)) : Prop :=
  ∃ C : Set (Subgroup (FreeGroup ℕ)), (∀ U ∈ C, IsFullyInvariant U ∧ V ≤ U) ∧
    IsChain (· ≤ ·) C ∧
    (∀ D : Set (Subgroup (FreeGroup ℕ)), (∀ U ∈ D, IsFullyInvariant U ∧ V ≤ U) →
      IsChain (· ≤ ·) D → C ⊆ D → D = C) ∧
    ∀ D ⊆ C, D.Nonempty → ∃ U ∈ D, ∀ U' ∈ D, U' ≤ U

/-- Kourovka 8.26 (A. V. Kuznetsov): there is a non-passable variety of groups. -/
def nonPassableVarietyExists : Prop :=
  ∃ V : Subgroup (FreeGroup ℕ), IsFullyInvariant V ∧ ¬ IsPassable V

/--
Resolved true: the Kourovka Archive answers 8.26 "Yes, there do", citing Anokhin (1996). (Every
variety generated by its finite groups is passable, as the problem notes.) Source: E. I. Khukhro and
V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue,
arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 8.26 (A. V. Kuznetsov); resolution:
M. I. Anokhin, A remark on lattices and the existence of impassable varieties of groups, Moscow
Univ. Math. Bull. 51, no. 1 (1996), 48-49, Kourovka Archive, Problem 8.26: Yes, there do (Anokhin),
https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.0300v49.
-/
public theorem_wanted nonPassableVarietyExists_holds : nonPassableVarietyExists

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.NonPassableVarietyKourovka8Problem26Wanted
